09:49:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re noticing a real effect—but it doesn’t mean gasoline has a permanently lower temperature than every other liquid. Gasoline’s **physical properties** make it *feel* unusually cold. The biggest reason is that gasoline is very volatile: it readily turns into vapor at room temperature. When a thin film gets on your skin, it evaporates quickly and takes heat from your skin to do so. That can cool your skin substantially, much faster than water or motor oil would. So evaporation is not a “trick”; it is an inherent property of gasoline. A few factors reinforce the effect: - **It spreads into a thin film** on skin, creating lots of surface area for evaporation. - **It evaporates much faster than water or oil.** Oil barely evaporates, so it mostly just sits there near its original temperature. - **Gas-station fuel is often stored in underground tanks**, where the temperature is relatively cool and stable—often cooler than sun-warmed containers, hoses, or tap water. - Your skin senses not just temperature, but **how rapidly heat leaves it**. Rapid evaporation can make gasoline feel cold even if a thermometer shows it started at about the same temperature as another liquid. If gasoline, water, and oil were sealed in identical containers in the same room long enough, a thermometer would show them reaching nearly the same temperature. But the instant gasoline is poured or touches skin, its rapid evaporation can make it feel much colder. Also, gasoline is hazardous to handle: it can irritate skin, its vapors are harmful and highly flammable, and it should be washed off with soap and water rather than left to evaporate on your skin.
