09:49:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your uncle’s observation can be completely valid in practice. Mechanics and people who handle fuel often say gasoline “runs cold” because, in real-world use, it very often *is* colder or produces a stronger cold sensation than other fluids. That happens because gasoline commonly: - comes from **large underground tanks**, which stay cooler and more stable than air-exposed containers; - **evaporates immediately** when poured or spilled, cooling the fuel left behind and anything it touches; - **spreads thinly** and draws heat from skin quickly; - is often handled from a hose, nozzle, or container that has also been cooled by the fuel. So “gasoline runs colder” is a useful shop-floor description of its behavior. The scientific distinction is just that gasoline does not have a built-in lower equilibrium temperature: if sealed gasoline, water, and oil sat in the same controlled room long enough, they would measure nearly the same temperature. But under normal handling conditions, gasoline is unusually good at *staying cool from storage and causing rapid cooling through evaporation*, so it reliably feels colder.
