09:50:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither. Experienced mechanics are describing a real, repeatable practical fact: gasoline often **is colder when handled**, and it almost always **feels colder** than water or oil. The distinction is between: 1. **“Gasoline is usually colder in use”** — often true. Gas-station fuel commonly comes from large underground tanks, where it can be cooler than outdoor air, sun-warmed equipment, or water sitting in a container. As it flows through a nozzle or sits in an open can, evaporation can cool it further. 2. **“Gasoline has an inherent temperature below other liquids in the same conditions”** — not true in the strict thermodynamic sense. Put sealed samples of gasoline, water, and oil in identical containers in the same temperature-controlled room long enough, and a thermometer will show them at essentially the same temperature. 3. **“Gasoline feels much colder at the same thermometer temperature”** — definitely true. It spreads and evaporates rapidly, removing heat from your skin. Your temperature sense responds strongly to that heat loss, not merely the liquid’s starting temperature. So your uncle isn’t “flat-out wrong.” “Gasoline runs cold” is useful mechanic shorthand for a genuine everyday behavior: fuel coming from its normal storage and exposed to air tends to be cool and produces unusually strong cooling. Science just explains *why* that happens and clarifies that it isn’t because gasoline has a permanently lower equilibrium temperature than other liquids.
