09:50:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Gasoline is lighter and thinner than water or most oils, but those traits do not by themselves make a liquid have a lower temperature. What they *do* contribute to is the effect you notice: - Gasoline flows and spreads into a thin layer easily. - It has weak intermolecular attraction compared with water, so its molecules escape into the air readily. - That rapid evaporation removes energy from the liquid and your skin. - Its low density and low viscosity make it feel “light” and help it spread, but the main cooling property is its high volatility. So it’s reasonable to say gasoline’s chemical nature makes it “run cold” in everyday use. But the relevant chemical feature is not simply that it is light or thin—it is that it evaporates readily and therefore causes strong cooling when exposed to air.
